      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Take \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2}+3} \\), so \\( f(n) \\) is the general term. On \\( [1, \\infty) \\), \\( f \\) is positive, continuous, and decreasing, so the integral test applies.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            1,
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "x",
                  2
               ],
               3
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The series starts at \\( n = 1 \\), so the matching integral is \\( \\int_{1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{x^{2}+3} \\, dx = \\lim_{b \\to \\infty} \\int_{1}^{b} \\frac{1}{x^{2}+3} \\, dx \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "An antiderivative is \\( F(x) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{3} \\right)}}{3} \\), so \\( \\int_{1}^{b} \\frac{1}{x^{2}+3} \\, dx = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3} b}{3} \\right)}}{3} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{18} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Divide",
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  [
                     "Sqrt",
                     3
                  ],
                  [
                     "Arctan",
